CORE
main.cpp
--cpp11
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ member class defined after: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
incomplete type
--
N5008 [temp.mem.class]/1, [temp.inst]/3: a member class of a class template
defined outside the class template is instantiated for S<char> when its
completeness is required, from the definition reachable then.  The front
end grafted the out-of-class definition into the class template only, so an
instance created before the definition kept the member class incomplete
("member operator got incomplete type on left hand side", bin53).  The
definition is now also instantiated for the existing instances.  This was the root cause of the dropped
basic_istream::read (cpp11_libstdcxx_header_drops/test_istream.desc):
<istream> explicitly instantiates basic_istream<char>::ignore before
istream.tcc defines basic_istream::sentry, and `if (__cerb)' then sees an
incomplete sentry with no operator bool.  g++ 13 and
clang++ 18 accept this program, and it fails only its WRONG assertion.
